The specific case that motivated this: a PathType enum was removed on
master and hardcoded to TO_END_NODE behavior. The resolution happened
to be correct, but it was done by reading the diff rather than the
commit message. The user pointed out the lesson holds even when you
get lucky — if the commit message had said something different, the
bot would have had no way to know it was wrong.

The CO/CT framing (our commit / their commit) gives the protocol
concrete handles. "Read both sides" is vague; "git show -q CO CT"
is not. The three steps are ordered by cost: commit messages are
cheap and often sufficient; the full diff is the last resort.

The existing "Inspect the incoming side as a commit" subsection covered
MERGE_HEAD commands but didn't impose an order or frame it as
mandatory. The CO/CT protocol does both.
